1|0@Windows10^1438450110#cto1yes:[deleted]$:To a degree we always under some sort of watch some OS definely are less telling that others. Firstly, if its a corporate computer or business computer most likely there is already software monitoring the computer via a 3rd party app that was put in by the IT of that business or corporation. Now in terms of a personal computer, internet activity is pretty much monitored by your ISP granted TPP at least from what I heard hasn't pass (yet, sadly) meaning ISPs are not required to monitor illegal activity or copyright infringement activities. Now end to end with VPN this can be a a problem if windows 10 is particularly forthcoming with in system activities because it would be the weakest link from what I seen so far most can be opped out and people found registry modifications to alleviate most other data-logging issues. Would I do all my most personal things on a windows 10 personally? Probably not, however for normal every day activities it gets the work done. I am pretty much on the fence though. To be fair I have multiple computers and my 2 major computers (work laptop and at home computer) (deals with work, gaming, video processing, network security, etc is still windows 7 (and linux tails, and debian). My purely dedicated gaming and multimedia computer is win 10. The default settings are concerning however turning them off and a bit of modifications seem to put me at ease for the moment. I'd be wary around the windows 10 computer though. I'd like to see news on windows 10 on the future about privacy audits though and anything that requires a high degree of privacy should be done in a highly secured OS (not much is known of windows 10 yet so wouldn't recommend windows 10 as a primary os)
